    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the probability that a randomly selected point \\((x, y, z)\\) within the cube \\([-1, 1]^3\\) lies within the unit sphere \\(\\mathcal{S}^2\\) centered at the origin with radius 1. This probability is given by the ratio of the volume of the sphere to the volume of the cube.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\n1. **Volume of the Cube:**\n   The cube has side length 2 (from -1 to 1), so its volume is:\n   \\[\n   V_{\\text{cube}} = 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 = 8\n   \\]\n\n2. **Volume of the Unit Sphere:**\n   The volume \\(V\\) of a sphere with radius \\(r\\) is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi r^3\n   \\]\n   For our unit sphere (\\(r = 1\\)):\n   \\[\n   V_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (1)^3 = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi\n   \\]\n\n3. **Probability Calculation:**\n   The probability \\(P\\) that a randomly selected point within the cube lies within the sphere is the ratio of the volume of the sphere to the volume of the cube:\n   \\[\n   P = \\frac{V_{\\text{sphere}}}{V_{\\text{cube}}} = \\frac{\\frac{4}{3} \\pi}{8} = \\frac{4 \\pi}{3 \\times 8} = \\frac{\\pi}{6}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Final Answer:**\n   Therefore, the probability that \\(x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \\le 1\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}\n   \\]",
