      "sample_index": 5,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the volume of the region where the point \\((x, y, z)\\) lies within the sphere of radius 1 centered at the origin, and then divide this volume by the volume of the cube defined by the limits \\(-1 \\le x \\le 1\\), \\(-1 \\le y \\le 1\\), and \\(-1 \\le z \\le 1\\).\n\n1. **Volume of the Cube**:\n   The cube has side length 2 (from \\(-1\\) to \\(1\\)), so its volume is:\n   \\[\n   V_{\\text{cube}} = 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 = 8\n   \\]\n\n2. **Volume of the Sphere**:\n   The sphere has radius 1, so its volume is:\n   \\[\n   V_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (1)^3 = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi\n   \\]\n\n3. **Probability Calculation**:\n   The probability that a randomly selected point \\((x, y, z)\\) within the cube lies within the sphere is the ratio of the volume of the sphere to the volume of the cube:\n   \\[\n   P = \\frac{V_{\\text{sphere}}}{V_{\\text{cube}}} = \\frac{\\frac{4}{3} \\pi}{8} = \\frac{4 \\pi}{24} = \\frac{\\pi}{6}\n   \\]\n\nLet's implement this in Python using sympy to confirm our solution.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the volume of the cube\nV_cube = 2 * 2 * 2\n\n# Define the volume of the sphere\nV_sphere = (4/3) * sp.pi * (1**3)\n\n# Calculate the probability\nP = V_sphere / V_cube\n\nprint(P)\n```\n```output\n0.523598775598299\n```\nThe output \\(0.523598775598299\\) is the numerical approximation of \\(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\). Therefore, the probability that a randomly selected point \\((x, y, z)\\) within the cube \\(-1 \\le x \\le 1\\), \\(-1 \\le y \\le 1\\), and \\(-1 \\le z \\le 1\\) lies within the sphere of radius 1 centered at the origin is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}\n\\]",
